### LLM Text in IETF Discussions
- **Presenter:** Stephen Farrell
- **Presentation:** [LLM Text in IETF Discussions](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rasprg-llm-text-in-ietf-discussions-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Stephen Farrell presented context and open research questions regarding LLM-generated text in email discussions, arguing for an IETF Best Current Practice (BCP). He suggested guidelines focusing on quoting or referencing LLM output rather than posting full LLM-generated messages, clearly distinguishing LLM text from human author text, and avoiding penalties for inadvertent non-compliance by newcomers.
  - Key concerns highlighted included:
    - **Reader Burden:** Senders cannot be assumed to fully understand or endorse LLM text, leading readers to skip large walls of text.
    - **Lack of Curiosity:** Statistical next-token generation lacks genuine human inquiry ("what did you mean?"), undermining interactive problem-solving.
    - **Infeasibility of Declarations:** Standardized declarations/headers are impractical due to complex local prompt setups.
    - **Mimicry & Deception:** Studies suggest humans begin mimicking LLM phrasing, and users frequently deny using LLMs when challenged.
  - Potential benefits include lowering language barriers for non-native English speakers if workflows like AI-assisted post-editing are structured properly.
  - Jay Daley noted LLMs rarely dismiss ideas out of hand, leading to volume, scope, and depth growth in list traffic. He inquired whether stigma or shame drives non-disclosure of LLM use.
  - Ignacio Castro raised the issue of indirect shaping, where summarizing mailing lists with LLMs alters human comprehension and confidence even when final messages are self-written.
  - Alvaro Retana channeled chat comments from Susan Hares regarding the IETF moderator team procedures, which hold posters responsible for LLM-generated text and treat repeated unhelpful postings as disruptive behavior.

### Beyond ASCII Art: Recruiting Protocol Experts for RFSeek User Studies
- **Presenter:** Noga Levy
- **Presentation:** [Beyond ASCII Art: Recruiting Protocol Experts for RFSeek User Studies](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rasprg-beyond-ascii-art-recruiting-protocol-experts-for-rfseek-user-studies-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Noga Levy presented an update on RFSeek, a tool that extracts protocol state machines grounded directly in RFC text to make logic auditable.
  - Demonstrated impact includes identifying a missing TCP transition (leading to an accepted erratum) and successfully reconstructing state models for DCCP, PPTB, QUIC, and TCP.
  - RFSeek is launching Zoom-based user studies to evaluate how human protocol experts inspect, refine, and correct state diagrams when LLM extraction errors occur.
  - Alvaro Retana encouraged broadcasting the call for volunteers to the RASPRG mailing list.

### Who Wrote the RFCs: Authorship, Affiliation and Community, 1969-2026
- **Presenters:** Carlos Kamienski and Marcelo Santos
- **Presentation:** [Who Wrote the RFCs: Authorship, Affiliation and Community, 1969-2026](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rasprg-who-wrote-the-rfcs-authorship-affiliation-and-community-1969-2026-00)
- **Discussion:**
  - Carlos Kamienski presented a historical dataset of 24,343 authorships across all RFCs from 1969 to 2026, compiled via Datatracker parsing, LLM assistance, and manual text curation.
  - Key historical trends identified:
    - **Affiliation Shift:** Academic authorship dropped from a majority during the early ARPANET era to 10–12% today, while corporate affiliations rose to ~76% (and independent authors to ~9%).
    - **Geographic Shift:** US-centric authorship diminished over time as international participation grew.
    - **Publication Gaps:** A drop in RFC publication between 1977 and 1982 corresponds to TCP/IP work being published as Internet Experiment Notes (IENs) rather than RFCs.
    - **Community Alignment:** Louvain community detection showed relatively weak overlap (AMI 0.36) between co-authorship clusters and formal working groups.
  - Jean Mahoney (RFC Production Center) clarified that canonicalization of organizational names (e.g., Cisco vs. Cisco Systems) is not enforced by the RPC, and noted country tags exist in modern XML RFC source files. She suggested coordinating data cleanup with the IETF Tools team.
  - Jay Daley and Lars Eggert observed that the symmetrical drop in academia and rise in corporate affiliations likely reflects individual researchers moving to industry alongside shifts in federal research funding (e.g., DARPA funding reductions and the rise of commercial Internet entities).

### Tracing Research into Standards: Investigating IRTF Contributions to the IETF
- **Presenter:** Michel Salis
- **Presentation:** [Tracing Research into Standards: Investigating IRTF Contributions to the IETF](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-rasprg-tracing-research-into-standards-investigating-irtf-contributions-to-the-ietf-02)
- **Discussion:**
  - Michel Salis introduced a six-stage methodology (Plan, Collect, Discover, Inspect, Review, Report) and an open web tool to trace how IRTF outputs transition into IETF standards.
  - Exploratory analysis across 34 active and inactive research groups revealed 1,618 document pairs sharing author email addresses and highlighted that formal references alone do not explain contribution depth.
  - Candidate categorization showed the Crypto Forum Research Group (CFRG) accounts for ~60% of identified IRTF-to-IETF candidate linkages.
  - Lars Eggert noted that CFRG is an organizational outlier due to its unique role in standardizing cryptographic algorithms for the IETF, suggesting it be analyzed distinctly from traditional research groups.
  - Ignacio Castro suggested testing the candidate discovery rules in reverse against known historic transitions (such as DTNRG to DTN WG).

